WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.469 --> 00:00:04.671
This is the FDA's food pyramid.

00:00:05.538 --> 00:00:07.774
For decades,
the predominant thought was that

00:00:07.774 --> 00:00:10.643
if you follow it,
you would be, quote unquote, "healthy".

00:00:11.044 --> 00:00:13.713
But does that pyramid really apply to
everyone?

00:00:14.481 --> 00:00:17.517
Nutrition is not a one-size-fits-
all approach.

00:00:17.517 --> 00:00:19.619
Neither is weight loss.
Neither is prevention

00:00:19.619 --> 00:00:20.987
of chronic conditions.

00:00:20.987 --> 00:00:24.090
And that's because we are all so different.

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:27.961
Eating is one of the most complex
social behaviors that we have.

00:00:29.295 --> 00:00:30.363
We have to do it.

00:00:30.363 --> 00:00:31.431
We love to do it.

00:00:31.431 --> 00:00:34.200
It's in our genes to want to eat.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:39.205
What we eat is extremely important
for our wellness, for our well-being.

00:00:39.305 --> 00:00:43.743
For our longevity, and not just ours,
but those of our families, too.

00:00:44.611 --> 00:00:48.348
And so we want to make sure
that the food choices that we make

00:00:48.348 --> 00:00:52.385
every day
support health rather than put

00:00:52.385 --> 00:00:55.455
us on a path to disease.

00:00:57.123 --> 00:00:58.458
There are hundreds of diets

00:00:58.458 --> 00:01:01.861
to choose from
from Atkins to Keto to Paleo.

00:01:02.395 --> 00:01:05.799
But the burgeoning field of Nutrogenomics
and personalized nutrition

00:01:05.999 --> 00:01:09.469
throws the idea of a diet
working for anyone out the window.

00:01:09.936 --> 00:01:14.074
Scientifically,
there is really nothing more intimate

00:01:14.074 --> 00:01:18.511
that our connection with food
and how it can affect our genes

00:01:19.379 --> 00:01:21.948
The idea of personalized nutrition

00:01:22.148 --> 00:01:24.918
is that we can discover

00:01:25.085 --> 00:01:28.621
what diet works best for our genes.

00:01:29.389 --> 00:01:31.624
I often tell my patients that your genes

00:01:31.624 --> 00:01:34.294
are not your destiny,
but they are your predisposition.

00:01:34.761 --> 00:01:39.199
Now we're delving a little further
and looking at our actual genetics.

00:01:39.599 --> 00:01:40.533
What were we born with?

00:01:40.533 --> 00:01:42.535
What are the genes we were born with?

00:01:42.535 --> 00:01:44.370
What are the genes we want to turn off?

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.506
What are the ones we want to turn on?

00:01:46.506 --> 00:01:49.609
How do we become a place where we know
our kids are going to grow up

00:01:49.943 --> 00:01:53.379
and have an easier time
knowing how to structure their lifestyle?

00:01:53.379 --> 00:01:56.416
Prescription to match longevity.

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:59.953
Nutrigenomics really looks at us as the individual.

00:02:11.131 --> 00:02:14.100
At the heart of Nutrigenomics
and precision nutrition --

00:02:14.334 --> 00:02:16.769
is understanding the human genome.

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:20.106
Genetically speaking, we are very similar.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:25.445
But if we look at those little outliers
outside of where we are similar,

00:02:25.945 --> 00:02:28.681
we have so many things
that make us different.

00:02:28.681 --> 00:02:31.184
Depending on which variants we carry.

00:02:31.384 --> 00:02:35.822
Of those genes, what flavors of the genes
we carry that may affect the way

00:02:35.822 --> 00:02:40.426
our brain, our body, our cells interact
with different nutrients.

00:02:40.927 --> 00:02:45.098
And those nutrients we consume can switch
certain genes on or off.

00:02:46.232 --> 00:02:50.737
So we have about 25,000 genes,
but in our brain cells,

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:54.440
a set of genes are on and a
 set of genes are off.

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:56.809
The genome, and looking at our DNA is really kind of

00:02:56.809 --> 00:02:59.646
what puts us aside from someone else

00:02:59.646 --> 00:03:03.383
and says, gosh, I'm more at risk for this,
or I would do better

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:06.452
lifting weights
than I would running a marathon.

00:03:06.686 --> 00:03:09.322
So that's where our DNA plays a role.

00:03:10.356 --> 00:03:11.958
So the genetic switches

00:03:11.958 --> 00:03:15.962
are very similar
to the switches of light in your room.

00:03:16.262 --> 00:03:19.632
That can control how much light you have,

00:03:19.632 --> 00:03:23.536
whether it's on or off
or whether it's dial up or down.

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:27.140
Nutrients essentially help the molecular machinery

00:03:27.373 --> 00:03:30.410
that can make these genetic switches easier

00:03:30.443 --> 00:03:32.979
or harder to turn on or off.

00:03:33.846 --> 00:03:36.449
And is there a possibility say,

00:03:36.449 --> 00:03:39.719
of giving people foods that are going to

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:43.356
affect those favorably.

00:03:45.191 --> 00:03:46.292
One of the most striking

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:48.261
examples of how food can drive biological

00:03:48.261 --> 00:03:50.496
processes by interacting with the genome 

00:03:50.496 --> 00:03:53.967
can be found, of all places, in a beehive.

00:03:55.668 --> 00:03:57.704
When we usually think of food,

00:03:57.770 --> 00:04:02.675
we think mostly about its calories
and nutrient content.

00:04:02.976 --> 00:04:06.579
But there is this whole other side of food

00:04:06.946 --> 00:04:10.750
that is much more intimately connected
with our biology,

00:04:11.084 --> 00:04:16.489
and that is the ability of nutrients
to essentially inform and shape

00:04:16.623 --> 00:04:20.159
our genome,
the blueprint that drives all life.

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:22.862
There are two kinds of bees...

00:04:22.862 --> 00:04:24.897
queen bees and worker bees.

00:04:25.131 --> 00:04:29.102
Queen bees give rise to the entire colony.

00:04:29.302 --> 00:04:30.703
Queen bees are fertile.

00:04:30.703 --> 00:04:33.339
They give rise
to every other bee in the colony.

00:04:33.573 --> 00:04:35.341
And they live for years.

00:04:35.341 --> 00:04:38.378
On the other hand,
worker bees are sterile.

00:04:38.745 --> 00:04:43.783
They don't have any offspring,
and they only live for a few weeks.

00:04:44.651 --> 00:04:47.954
What you might not know
is that actually worker bees

00:04:47.954 --> 00:04:51.291
and the queen bee
are genetically identical organisms.

00:04:51.624 --> 00:04:55.328
What shapes them into these
two completely different life forms

00:04:55.495 --> 00:04:57.430
is actually the food they eat.

00:04:57.430 --> 00:05:00.033
The queen bee, when she's a larva,

00:05:00.066 --> 00:05:03.303
she feeds on this food called Royal Jelly.

00:05:03.603 --> 00:05:07.006
On the other hand,
the worker bees mostly eat pollen...

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:12.845
and this food that they get when they're
developing determines their physiology.

00:05:13.012 --> 00:05:15.415
And their anatomy
for the rest of their lives.

00:05:19.152 --> 00:05:20.753
Obviously, humans are not bees.

00:05:20.753 --> 00:05:24.691
But the amino acid methionene influences

00:05:24.691 --> 00:05:26.926
genetic dials in humans and mice

00:05:26.926 --> 00:05:29.195
Just like royal jelly does with bees.

00:05:29.696 --> 00:05:34.600
Methionine can be used to add
a little tag

00:05:34.867 --> 00:05:38.604
to the genetic switches
or even to the genetic blueprint.

00:05:38.905 --> 00:05:43.676
And what this tag does, essentially
it makes it harder

00:05:43.676 --> 00:05:49.615
for the genes to be turned on and harder
for the genetic switches to be accessed.

00:05:49.849 --> 00:05:53.419
And so it's very important to have a diet

00:05:53.419 --> 00:05:55.955
where we have a balanced level on methionine

00:05:55.955 --> 00:06:01.294
so that the genetic switches
that are to be off remain off.

00:06:02.562 --> 00:06:05.465
Ultimately, this information
hinges on dietitians

00:06:05.465 --> 00:06:08.668
and doctors applying this information
to the real world.

00:06:09.736 --> 00:06:11.704
I never want my patients to look at a gene

00:06:11.704 --> 00:06:14.707
and say, "Well, I have the gene,
so I might as well just enjoy life."

00:06:14.741 --> 00:06:17.410
Instead, I want them to look at it
and say, I have the gene.

00:06:17.744 --> 00:06:23.349
What can I know from a Nutrigenomics
perspective to alter my lifestyle

00:06:23.349 --> 00:06:25.651
so that this gene doesn't rear
its ugly head?

00:06:26.386 --> 00:06:29.689
In terms of prevention, of chronic
conditions, in terms of weight management,

00:06:29.689 --> 00:06:34.160
it's really taking our general sense
and knowledge of the evidence

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:38.264
based approaches to provide a diet
that makes sense.

00:06:38.698 --> 00:06:40.333
Some individuals have genetics

00:06:40.333 --> 00:06:44.070
that make them more prone
to be deficient in folate or in B12.

00:06:44.404 --> 00:06:46.906
So we have to really focus on those two

00:06:46.939 --> 00:06:50.676
When we're sitting face to face
with a client or a patient and saying,

00:06:50.676 --> 00:06:54.280
OK, listen, I don't think you're
getting enough folate and you are

00:06:54.313 --> 00:06:58.718
genetically predisposed
to not being able to absorb enough of it.

00:06:58.718 --> 00:07:00.787
So let's talk
about how we get that in our diet.

00:07:01.988 --> 00:07:04.357
And diets based off genetic information.

00:07:04.357 --> 00:07:07.794
Could lead to disease prevention
that we haven't seen before.

00:07:08.761 --> 00:07:12.665
We know that people
that have a type of untreatable epilepsy

00:07:12.999 --> 00:07:16.903
can actually live relatively good life

00:07:16.903 --> 00:07:19.338
by eating a ketogenic diet.

00:07:20.139 --> 00:07:24.844
This idea that we can couple
a certain diet to treat a disease

00:07:24.844 --> 00:07:29.949
is now one of the main avenue of
investigation for the treatment of cancer.

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:34.353
There are many cancers
that are caused by diet right.

00:07:35.254 --> 00:07:37.757
For example, for colon cancer,

00:07:38.257 --> 00:07:42.962
scientists know that amino acid
serine is very important.

00:07:43.162 --> 00:07:47.433
And so in addition, to treating the cancer
with the kind of therapeutics

00:07:47.433 --> 00:07:51.671
that are used for that cancers,
the idea is like, what if now

00:07:51.871 --> 00:07:55.842
we can deprive people of these amino acids

00:07:56.042 --> 00:07:59.545
in ways that essentially make
the cancer cells more vulnerable?

00:07:59.912 --> 00:08:01.247
Take two cod fillets.

00:08:01.247 --> 00:08:03.349
Drizzled of olive oil, jerk seasoning,

00:08:03.916 --> 00:08:07.286
give it wrap,
and that right there is lean in 15

00:08:09.555 --> 00:08:13.693
Nutrigenomics and precision nutrition
is one of those rare scientific fields

00:08:13.893 --> 00:08:17.263
that overlaps with the Instagram
friendly wellness industry.

00:08:18.197 --> 00:08:22.301
There is a lot of interest
in this idea of personalized nutrition

00:08:22.301 --> 00:08:26.672
and especially how accessible it is
to the public.

00:08:27.039 --> 00:08:31.711
You can go on Google
and type 'personalized nutrition'

00:08:31.711 --> 00:08:36.115
or diet and genes, and you will find

00:08:36.215 --> 00:08:39.485
dozens of different companies
that offer this

00:08:39.485 --> 00:08:42.555
nutrition precision tests.

00:08:42.555 --> 00:08:44.590
One of those companies is Rootine.

00:08:45.324 --> 00:08:48.661
founded to get customers to take vitamins
more efficiently.

00:08:49.428 --> 00:08:50.496
I'm Rachel Sanders.

00:08:50.496 --> 00:08:53.065
I'm CEO and co-founder of Rootine.

00:08:53.599 --> 00:08:56.469
Rootine is a company
that focuses on optimizing

00:08:56.469 --> 00:08:59.305
health and human performance
with precision nutrition.

00:08:59.472 --> 00:09:03.676
Our members come to us because they're
looking for really focusing on

00:09:03.843 --> 00:09:07.680
total body health and improving in areas
more common health conditions.

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:12.952
Like stress, fatigue, brain fog, immune
health support, need balance and more.

00:09:13.219 --> 00:09:17.356
And they really come to us
because we have a tailored approach

00:09:17.356 --> 00:09:18.658
and we create tailored

00:09:18.658 --> 00:09:22.628
products specific to their individual
needs and individual biology.

00:09:25.631 --> 00:09:26.933
While many studies

00:09:26.933 --> 00:09:31.070
have found vitamins and supplements
don't do much to improve overall health

00:09:31.437 --> 00:09:34.807
Rootine claims that they're personalized
tests stemming from sciences

00:09:34.807 --> 00:09:38.244
like Nutrigenomics
make their supplements different.

00:09:39.211 --> 00:09:42.748
The supplement industry in general
has a lot of skeptics.

00:09:43.182 --> 00:09:47.620
We don't use kind of new ingredients
with very little research behind them.

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:50.856
Trying to make claims
or have specific results

00:09:50.856 --> 00:09:54.327
based on specific interventions
is definitely difficult.

00:09:54.894 --> 00:09:58.998
But Rootine claims their personalized
tests see positive results.

00:09:59.465 --> 00:10:03.035
Over 80% of our members see improvement
in at least one health metric...

00:10:03.035 --> 00:10:06.505
Things like improvement in energy
levels, lower stress,

00:10:07.073 --> 00:10:09.175
as well as not immune immune sleep.

00:10:09.809 --> 00:10:14.313
But even with the claims from Rootine
as well as dozens of other personalized

00:10:14.313 --> 00:10:18.584
nutrition companies,
the science still has a long way to go.

00:10:19.518 --> 00:10:22.922
The thing about longevity
is that it takes a long time to study.

00:10:22.989 --> 00:10:24.757
That's why we call longevity.

00:10:24.757 --> 00:10:27.927
When it comes to Nutrigenomics,
we are learning every day.

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:32.498
When we started doing Nutrigenomics,
we had about seven genes

00:10:32.498 --> 00:10:34.634
that we were going over with
with patients.

00:10:34.634 --> 00:10:36.535
Now we have about 70.

00:10:36.535 --> 00:10:39.438
Well, some people really believe that once

00:10:39.438 --> 00:10:43.743
we can map the entirety 
of food nutrient interactions

00:10:44.143 --> 00:10:48.447
and map them in a way
that's inclusive of different sexes,

00:10:48.981 --> 00:10:51.317
ethnicities, lifestyles

00:10:51.584 --> 00:10:56.188
than we can arrive to this personalized,
precise nutrition.

00:10:56.522 --> 00:11:00.359
But on the other hand,
this question is so complicated

00:11:00.593 --> 00:11:04.330
that it might take
a very long time for us to get the answer.

00:11:06.565 --> 00:11:09.702
But ultimately
Nutrigenomics isn't going anywhere,

00:11:09.902 --> 00:11:14.140
and it may be the way forward
with how we think about the food we eat.

00:11:14.473 --> 00:11:18.244
People are no longer interested in diets.

00:11:18.644 --> 00:11:23.382
They're interested in dietary patterns
that speak more to who they are

00:11:23.382 --> 00:11:27.687
and embrace who they are personally
and genetically sociologically.

00:11:28.387 --> 00:11:31.557
It's hard to argue against our own genetics

00:11:31.557 --> 00:11:34.760
in terms of determining
what's the best dietary approach for us.

00:11:35.394 --> 00:11:36.929
People are looking for what works.

00:11:36.929 --> 00:11:40.366
They want to live longer, but people
don't want to just live longer anymore.

00:11:40.366 --> 00:11:41.333
They want to live better.

